= Polysemy =

== Overview ==
'''Polysemy''' is the phenomenon in which one linguistic form is associated with multiple related senses or interpretations.

For example, ''mouth'' can refer to a person's mouth, the opening of a cave, or the place where a river enters another body of water. These uses are not identical, but they are related.

== Polysemy and homonymy ==
Polysemy is commonly contrasted with '''homonymy'''.

A homonymous form has meanings treated as unrelated, such as ''bank'' for a financial institution and ''bank'' beside a river.

The distinction is useful but not always sharp. Historical origin, speaker intuitions, dictionary practice, and synchronic semantic structure can point in different directions.

== Polysemy and ambiguity ==
[[Ambiguity]] is broader.

Polysemy can create multiple interpretations, but not every ambiguity is polysemy. Structural ambiguity, scope ambiguity, and unrelated homonymy are different phenomena.

== Regular polysemy ==
Some sense alternations recur across many lexical items.

Examples can include animal ↔ meat, institution ↔ building, container ↔ contents, creator ↔ work, and process ↔ result.

Regularity motivates theories that derive related senses rather than listing every reading independently.

== Contextual interpretation ==
[[Context (linguistics)|Context]] affects which sense is active or salient.

This does not settle whether the senses are separately represented in the lexicon, generated by rules, inferred pragmatically, or emerge from contextualized conceptual representations.

== Why polysemy matters ==
Polysemy matters to [[Lexical semantics]], lexicography, language acquisition, psycholinguistics, translation, information retrieval, word-sense disambiguation, and distributional/contextual language models.

== Epistemic notes ==
There is no universal criterion for counting senses. A dictionary's sense divisions are useful editorial analyses, not direct measurements of discrete mental objects.

